1. alloc(index=0, size=64, data=AAAA)                // chunk_A allocated 
2. realloc(index=0, size=0)                          // chunk_A freed, ptr to chunk_A still referencable (UAF)
3. realloc(index=0. size=64, data=AAAAAAAABBBBBBBB)  // chunk_A returned in a freed state, gives write ability into tcache_entry
// 4. realloc(index=0, size=0)                          // chunk_A is double freed - GLIBC 2.29 mitigation is passed 

The reason this works is that realloc(ptr, size) when given size > 0 and less than original size simply checks the existing size
of the chunk. Because the old chunk size is enough to satisfy the request - it just returns the same ptr value back. realloc does
not check if the chunk was already in tcache 'freed state'. So realloc does not affect the freed state of the chunk.

Arbitrary write
In step 3 overwrite both the *next and *key entries in tcache_entry for chunk_A in tcache. The *next gives the next addr to return
as the chunk. Thus 2 allocations are required - first one chunk_A then target (overwritten *next)
  Tcache: chunk_A -> sabotaged *next
  heap (var): [chunk_A dangling pointer, 0x0]

However simply allocating does not work. allocating index 1 means that the heap list is full and further allocations are not 
feasible. We cannot free either index 0 which holds the dangling pointer nor index 1 which holds chunk_A.

In order to allocate twice - with the restrictions of the program, we can do the following. index 1 allocates chunk_A. The tcache 
is now only pointing to the sabotaged *next. We can realloc index 1 to a larger size and then free index 1. This puts chunk back
into tcache but not into the size of chunk_A and the sabotaged *next. Index 1 can then alloc the same size as chunk_A which returns
the sabotaged *next value. 


